| EM OV TLS CA - G3B (eMudhra BV)   | A012308 | emSign Root TLS CA - G3 | 98B896625C0C230ADEEC3EB2CF6AAD5001ECF9156A68644E82374CE5833BC25B |
| EM DV TLS CA - G3B (eMudhra BV)   | A012302 | emSign Root TLS CA - G3 | 42C4ACCD12C4F59597925E1A08A7A90102608543809308D50EC427E118BA1493 |
| EM EV TLS CA - G3C (eMudhra Inc)  | A012296 | emSign Root TLS CA - G3 | 432EC25F0D28DE9006D97BF752F17BBB2CD41F669933FCB072D9A1148D0261F3 |
| EM OV TLS CA - G3C (eMudhra Inc)  | A012300 | emSign Root TLS CA - G3 | CB6E15165AC802E9B3F993F16C25D41F4E81C21EBDE71AE1B012AC00BF9C7C3F |
| EM DV TLS CA - G3C (eMudhra Inc)  | A012294 | emSign Root TLS CA - G3 | 0164D7CEAE5BB909C272AF151FF44B1177AE9B47D9424B94F1D9558C30934D19 |
| EM OV CS CA - G3B (eMudhra BV)    | A012306 | emSign Root CS CA - G3  | 9E39DE2881273ACC225A149C72BA3E0F51424963669AB633EE1DC7DF5DB067B4 |
| EM OV CS CA - G3C (eMudhra Inc)   | A012298 | emSign Root CS CA - G3  | 4B2460A466F95FD40113BCDCCA2CEB29B3B093921DE45474DA7DF48874C630B1 |

The list lives in the `EmudraWeakKeys::compromised_certs` option and can
be extended via `redef` or the config framework. Keys are the lowercase
hex SHA-256 hash of the DER-encoded certificate.

Usage
-----

    zkg install emudra-weak-keys

or load the scripts directly:

    zeek -r trace.pcap /path/to/emudra-weak-keys/scripts

The package checks every certificate that Zeek's X509 analyzer parses
(the `x509_certificate` event). Certificates are matched on the SHA-256
hash of their DER encoding as re-encoded by OpenSSL, so data appended
after the certificate does not change the hash. If
`X509::hash_function` is the default `sha256_hash`, the fingerprint
computed by Zeek's base scripts is reused; otherwise the package
computes the SHA-256 hash itself.

When a match is found, a notice of type
`EmudraWeakKeys::Compromised_Intermediate_Cert` is raised, with `fuid`
referring to the matching certificate. For certificates seen in SSL/TLS
(server and client chains), the message names the matching
intermediate and its position in the chain, and the `sub` field
contains the leaf certificate subject, the SNI (if present) and the
SHA-256 hash. Notices are suppressed for one day per server address,
port and certificate.

Since the certificate is checked as soon as it is parsed, detection
does not depend on the TLS handshake completing; clients that already
distrust these intermediates will typically abort the connection.

Limitations
-----------

- Certificates are matched by their exact SHA-256 hash. Cross-signed
  variants of these intermediates (e.g. the ones signed by Sectigo)
  have different hashes and are only detected if their hashes are added
  to `EmudraWeakKeys::compromised_certs`.
- Only certificates that Zeek passes to the X509 analyzer are checked:
  SSL/TLS and DTLS chains, as well as certificates in RDP and Kerberos
  (PKINIT). Certificates transferred as files (e.g. downloaded over
  HTTP) or embedded in OCSP responses are not checked.
- In TLS 1.3 the certificate chain is encrypted and cannot be seen by
  Zeek, so detection only works for TLS 1.2 and earlier (unless
  decryption is used).
- A server that omits the intermediate from the chain it sends will not
  be detected.
- The two code-signing CAs will usually not appear in TLS traffic.
